1
00:00:07,780 --> 00:00:13,900
And the sun leapt up, leaving the beautiful water,
into the bronze-bright heaven, to shine for the immortals

2
00:00:13,900 --> 00:00:17,580
and for mortal men across the grain-giving plowland;

3
00:00:17,580 --> 00:00:21,660
and they came in to Pylos,
the strong-built city of Neleus.

4
00:00:21,660 --> 00:00:25,140
There on the beach of the sea
the people were offering victims,

5
00:00:25,140 --> 00:00:28,440
bulls all black, to the earth-shaker
with the blue-black hair.

6
00:00:28,440 --> 00:00:32,760
Nine tiers of seats there were,
five hundred sitting in each,

7
00:00:32,760 --> 00:00:35,840
and before each company nine bulls were held ready.

8
00:00:35,840 --> 00:00:40,020
They had just tasted the innards,
and were burning thigh-cuts for the god,

9
00:00:40,020 --> 00:00:43,860
when the ship came straight in:
they brailed the sails of the trim vessel,

10
00:00:43,860 --> 00:00:48,180
furled them aloft, moored her,
and stepped ashore themselves;

11
00:00:48,180 --> 00:00:51,920
and Telemachus stepped from the ship,
Athena going before him.

12
00:00:51,920 --> 00:00:55,180
The goddess spoke to him first, gray-eyed Athena:

13
00:00:55,180 --> 00:00:59,100
"Telemachus, no more need of
shyness now, not a scrap of it:

14
00:00:59,100 --> 00:01:01,480
this is why you have sailed the sea — to learn news

15
00:01:01,480 --> 00:01:04,680
of your father: where the earth
hides him, what doom he met.

16
00:01:04,680 --> 00:01:08,140
Come then, go straight to Nestor, breaker of horses:

17
00:01:08,140 --> 00:01:11,100
let us see what counsel he keeps hidden in his breast.

18
00:01:11,100 --> 00:01:14,340
Beg him yourself, so that he speaks unerring truth.

19
00:01:14,340 --> 00:01:18,180
He will tell you no lie:
he is clear-headed beyond most men."

20
00:01:18,180 --> 00:01:20,400
Then clear-headed Telemachus answered her:

21
00:01:20,400 --> 00:01:23,280
"Mentor, how am I to go up? How greet him?

22
00:01:23,280 --> 00:01:28,760
I have no practice yet in close-woven speech —
and a young man is shy of questioning his elder."

23
00:01:28,760 --> 00:01:32,060
Then the goddess spoke to him again, gray-eyed Athena:

24
00:01:32,060 --> 00:01:36,780
"Telemachus, some of it your own heart will see,
and some a god will prompt. For I do not think

25
00:01:36,780 --> 00:01:39,500
you were born and raised without the gods' good will."

26
00:01:39,500 --> 00:01:44,860
So speaking, Pallas Athena led the way swiftly,
and he followed in the footsteps of the god.

27
00:01:44,860 --> 00:01:48,820
They came to the gathering-place of
the men of Pylos, the rows of seats,

28
00:01:48,820 --> 00:01:51,600
where Nestor sat with his sons, and around them companions

29
00:01:51,600 --> 00:01:55,400
were readying the feast,
roasting meats and skewering others.

30
00:01:55,400 --> 00:01:58,740
But when they saw the strangers they came all in a crowd,

31
00:01:58,740 --> 00:02:01,800
clasped their hands in welcome, and bade them be seated.

32
00:02:01,800 --> 00:02:07,760
First Peisistratus, Nestor's son, coming near,
took the hands of both, and settled them at the feast

33
00:02:07,760 --> 00:02:12,960
on soft fleeces over the sand of the sea,
beside his brother Thrasymedes and his father;

34
00:02:12,960 --> 00:02:19,040
and he gave them portions of the innards, and poured
wine into a golden cup, and pledging her he addressed

35
00:02:19,040 --> 00:02:22,360
Pallas Athena, daughter of aegis-bearing Zeus:

36
00:02:22,360 --> 00:02:27,560
"Pray now, stranger, to the lord Poseidon,
for his is the feast you have found in coming here.

37
00:02:27,560 --> 00:02:32,840
And when you have poured and prayed, as is right and
custom, give this man too the cup of honey-sweet wine

38
00:02:32,840 --> 00:02:36,860
to pour, since he also, I think,
prays to the deathless ones:

39
00:02:36,860 --> 00:02:38,360
all men have need of the gods.

40
00:02:38,360 --> 00:02:44,220
But he is the younger, my own age to the year,
and so to you first I give the golden cup."

41
00:02:44,220 --> 00:02:50,580
With that he set in her hand the cup of sweet wine;
and Athena rejoiced in the man — clear-headed, just —

42
00:02:50,580 --> 00:02:56,660
because to her first he gave the golden cup;
and at once she prayed at length to the lord Poseidon:

43
00:02:56,660 --> 00:03:03,120
"Hear me, Poseidon who hold the earth, and do not
grudge us who pray the accomplishment of these works.

44
00:03:03,120 --> 00:03:09,440
To Nestor first of all, and to his sons, grant glory,
and then to all the rest give gracious recompense,

45
00:03:09,440 --> 00:03:12,180
to all the Pylians, for this splendid hecatomb.

46
00:03:12,180 --> 00:03:15,680
And grant besides that Telemachus and
I sail home with the thing accomplished

47
00:03:15,680 --> 00:03:18,660
we came here for, in our fast black ship."

48
00:03:18,660 --> 00:03:21,520
So she prayed — and she herself was accomplishing it all.

49
00:03:21,520 --> 00:03:26,980
Then she gave Telemachus the beautiful two-handled cup,
and the dear son of Odysseus prayed the same.

50
00:03:26,980 --> 00:03:31,540
And when they had roasted the
outer meats and drawn them off,

51
00:03:31,540 --> 00:03:35,440
they divided the portions and feasted the glorious feast.

52
00:03:35,440 --> 00:03:38,760
But when they had put away desire for food and drink,

53
00:03:38,760 --> 00:03:42,060
Gerenian Nestor the horseman began to speak among them:

54
00:03:42,060 --> 00:03:48,640
"Now is the fairer time to inquire and ask our guests
who they are, now they have had their joy of food.

55
00:03:48,640 --> 00:03:52,880
Strangers, who are you?
From where do you sail the watery ways?

56
00:03:52,880 --> 00:03:58,080
Is it on some business — or do you rove at
random, the way sea-raiders do, who wander

57
00:03:58,080 --> 00:04:02,100
staking their own lives, bearing evil to strangers?"

58
00:04:02,100 --> 00:04:08,060
Then clear-headed Telemachus answered him, emboldened:
Athena herself had set courage in his heart,

59
00:04:08,060 --> 00:04:13,780
so that he might ask about his absent father
and so that good report among men might be his:

60
00:04:13,780 --> 00:04:19,940
"Nestor, son of Neleus, great glory of the Achaeans,
you ask from where we are, and I will tell you.

61
00:04:19,940 --> 00:04:26,140
We have come from Ithaca, under Mount Neion;
and the business I speak of is private, not the people's.

62
00:04:26,140 --> 00:04:30,380
I follow the wide fame of my father,
in hope I may hear of him:

63
00:04:30,380 --> 00:04:36,500
noble, enduring-hearted Odysseus, who once, they say,
fought at your side when you sacked the Trojans' city.

64
00:04:36,500 --> 00:04:39,600
All the others, all who warred against the Trojans,

65
00:04:39,600 --> 00:04:43,140
we have had word of —
where each man died his bitter death;

66
00:04:43,140 --> 00:04:47,000
but his death the son of Cronos has left past finding:

67
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:51,440
no one can say for certain where he perished,
whether he went down on the mainland at enemy hands

68
00:04:51,440 --> 00:04:54,940
or on the deep, under the waves of Amphitrite.

69
00:04:54,940 --> 00:05:00,380
That is why I come now to your knees: in hope you will
consent to tell his bitter death — whether you saw it

70
00:05:00,380 --> 00:05:03,900
with your own eyes, or heard the tale of him from another

71
00:05:03,900 --> 00:05:06,960
wanderer. Beyond all men his mother bore him for sorrow.

72
00:05:06,960 --> 00:05:09,900
Do not soften anything from courtesy or pity:

73
00:05:09,900 --> 00:05:12,680
tell me fully whatever sight you came upon.

74
00:05:12,680 --> 00:05:18,180
I beg you — if ever my father, good Odysseus,
promised you word or work and made it good

75
00:05:18,180 --> 00:05:21,980
in the Trojans' country,
where you Achaeans suffered your pains,

76
00:05:21,980 --> 00:05:25,120
remember it for me now: tell me the unerring truth."

77
00:05:25,120 --> 00:05:27,940
Then Gerenian Nestor the horseman answered him:

78
00:05:27,940 --> 00:05:34,100
"Dear boy — since you bring back the misery we endured
in that country, we headstrong sons of the Achaeans,

79
00:05:34,100 --> 00:05:39,240
all our roving in ships across the misted
sea chasing plunder wherever Achilles led,

80
00:05:39,240 --> 00:05:44,540
and all our fighting around the great city of lord Priam —
there, in those years, our best were killed.

81
00:05:44,540 --> 00:05:50,880
There lies Ajax, dear to the war-god; there Achilles;
there Patroclus, a counselor the equal of the gods;

82
00:05:50,880 --> 00:05:57,800
there my own dear son, strong and without fault,
Antilochus — surpassing swift of foot, and a fighter.

83
00:05:57,800 --> 00:06:02,560
And many other evils we suffered besides:
who among mortal men could tell them all?

84
00:06:02,560 --> 00:06:07,980
Not if you stayed five years — six years —
asking all the evils the noble Achaeans suffered there:

85
00:06:07,980 --> 00:06:11,100
you would go home to your own country worn out first.

86
00:06:11,100 --> 00:06:13,640
Nine years we stitched evils around them, plotting

87
00:06:13,640 --> 00:06:18,200
with every kind of cunning — and the son
of Cronos barely brought it to an end.

88
00:06:18,200 --> 00:06:24,180
And there no man would set his wits against him openly —
willing, that is — for noble Odysseus won by far,

89
00:06:24,180 --> 00:06:30,360
in every kind of cunning: your father — if truly
you are his blood. Awe takes me, looking at you.

90
00:06:30,360 --> 00:06:35,180
Your speech, at least, is his: you would not
think a younger man could speak so like him.

91
00:06:35,180 --> 00:06:40,660
In all that time, I and noble Odysseus never
spoke on opposite sides in assembly or in council:

92
00:06:40,660 --> 00:06:43,500
one heart between us — with sense and considered counsel

93
00:06:43,500 --> 00:06:46,840
we planned for the Argives how the best might come about.

94
00:06:46,840 --> 00:06:51,980
But when we had sacked Priam's steep city,
and gone to our ships, and a god scattered the Achaeans,

95
00:06:51,980 --> 00:06:54,380
then, then Zeus devised in his heart a bitter homecoming

96
00:06:54,380 --> 00:07:00,520
for the Argives — since not all of us were men of sense
or of justice. So many of them came to an evil end

97
00:07:00,520 --> 00:07:03,860
through the ruinous wrath of the
gray-eyed daughter of the mighty Father —

98
00:07:03,860 --> 00:07:06,520
who set strife between the two sons of Atreus.

99
00:07:06,520 --> 00:07:12,020
The two of them called the Achaeans all to assembly —
rashly, in no due order, at the setting of the sun,

100
00:07:12,020 --> 00:07:14,620
and the sons of the Achaeans came heavy with wine —

101
00:07:14,620 --> 00:07:17,400
and they spoke out the reason they had gathered the army.

102
00:07:17,400 --> 00:07:22,460
There Menelaus urged all the Achaeans to remember
homecoming across the broad back of the sea;

103
00:07:22,460 --> 00:07:28,540
and it did not please Agamemnon, not at all:
he wanted to hold the army back, and offer holy hecatombs

104
00:07:28,540 --> 00:07:34,240
to heal the terrible anger of Athena — the fool.
He did not know she was not to be persuaded:

105
00:07:34,240 --> 00:07:38,020
the mind of the gods who are
forever does not turn so fast.

106
00:07:38,020 --> 00:07:42,320
So the two of them stood trading hard words;
and the strong-greaved Achaeans sprang up

107
00:07:42,320 --> 00:07:45,740
with a huge din, and the counsel split them two ways.

108
00:07:45,740 --> 00:07:48,060
That night we lay on our anger, brooding hard thoughts

109
00:07:48,060 --> 00:07:51,540
against each other — for Zeus
was readying the stroke of evil —

110
00:07:51,540 --> 00:07:55,340
and at dawn half of us hauled our
ships to the bright salt water

111
00:07:55,340 --> 00:07:58,760
and loaded our goods aboard, and the deep-girdled women.

112
00:07:58,760 --> 00:08:03,560
Half the army held back, staying there with
Atreus' son Agamemnon, shepherd of the people;

113
00:08:03,560 --> 00:08:06,680
and half of us embarked and rowed. And the ships ran fast,

114
00:08:06,680 --> 00:08:10,040
for a god smoothed the sea over its monstrous deeps.

115
00:08:10,040 --> 00:08:13,120
Coming to Tenedos, we offered victims to the gods,

116
00:08:13,120 --> 00:08:16,720
aching for home. But Zeus had
no thought yet of homecoming —

117
00:08:16,720 --> 00:08:20,240
iron-hearted, he stirred up evil strife a second time.

118
00:08:20,240 --> 00:08:22,940
Some turned their curved ships around and went back —

119
00:08:22,940 --> 00:08:27,140
those with Odysseus, the battle-wise lord,
the man of shifting counsels —

120
00:08:27,140 --> 00:08:30,260
carrying favor once again to Atreus' son Agamemnon.

121
00:08:30,260 --> 00:08:36,640
But I, with the ships that followed me gathered close,
fled on, for I knew the god was devising evils.

122
00:08:36,640 --> 00:08:40,760
And the warlike son of Tydeus fled,
and roused his companions.

123
00:08:40,760 --> 00:08:46,360
Late upon our track came fair-haired Menelaus,
and caught us at Lesbos debating the long crossing:

124
00:08:46,360 --> 00:08:51,540
whether to sail above rugged Chios,
by the isle of Psyria, keeping Chios on our left,

125
00:08:51,540 --> 00:08:54,820
or under Chios, past windy Mimas.

126
00:08:54,820 --> 00:09:01,740
We asked the god to show a sign, and he showed one,
and bade us cut across the middle sea to Euboea,

127
00:09:01,740 --> 00:09:04,800
to run out from under the evil quickest.

128
00:09:04,800 --> 00:09:10,660
A whistling wind sprang up astern, and the ships
ran fast down the fish-filled ways, and to Geraestus

129
00:09:10,660 --> 00:09:14,600
they came in by night. And to Poseidon we laid many

130
00:09:14,600 --> 00:09:17,500
bulls' thighs on the fire, having measured the great gulf.

131
00:09:17,500 --> 00:09:25,500
It was the fourth day when in Argos the companions of
Diomedes, breaker of horses, moored their trim ships;

132
00:09:25,500 --> 00:09:31,240
but I held on for Pylos, and the wind never
failed from the day the god first sent it blowing.

133
00:09:31,240 --> 00:09:35,800
So I came home, dear child,
knowing nothing — I cannot tell you

134
00:09:35,800 --> 00:09:38,460
who of the Achaeans were saved and who were lost.

135
00:09:38,460 --> 00:09:44,740
But all I hear, sitting here in our halls,
you shall learn, as is right; I will hide nothing.

136
00:09:44,740 --> 00:09:48,800
The Myrmidons, they say,
those furious spearmen, came home safe,

137
00:09:48,800 --> 00:09:55,660
led by the shining son of great-hearted Achilles;
safe too Philoctetes, Poias' splendid son;

138
00:09:55,660 --> 00:10:02,540
and Idomeneus brought all his companions to Crete,
all who escaped the war: the sea took none from him.

139
00:10:02,540 --> 00:10:05,880
And Atreus' son — you have heard
yourselves, far off as you live,

140
00:10:05,880 --> 00:10:10,140
how he came home, and how
Aegisthus plotted his bitter death.

141
00:10:10,140 --> 00:10:13,540
But that man paid for it — grimly, in full.

142
00:10:13,540 --> 00:10:16,780
How good a thing it is,
that a son of the dead man be left!

143
00:10:16,780 --> 00:10:20,140
For that son took vengeance on the killer of his father,

144
00:10:20,140 --> 00:10:23,840
Aegisthus of the crooked counsels,
who cut down his famous father.

145
00:10:23,840 --> 00:10:27,320
You too, my friend — and I see how tall and fine you are —

146
00:10:27,320 --> 00:10:31,500
be brave, so that men not yet
born will speak well of you."

147
00:10:31,500 --> 00:10:33,940
Then clear-headed Telemachus answered him:

148
00:10:33,940 --> 00:10:36,940
"Nestor, son of Neleus, great glory of the Achaeans,

149
00:10:36,940 --> 00:10:40,260
yes — that man took his vengeance
in full, and the Achaeans

150
00:10:40,260 --> 00:10:44,600
will carry his fame wide,
a thing for men to come to hear of.

151
00:10:44,600 --> 00:10:50,260
If only the gods would clothe me in strength like that,
to pay the suitors back for their galling trespass —

152
00:10:50,260 --> 00:10:53,560
the insolent men who scheme their
reckless outrage against me!

153
00:10:53,560 --> 00:10:56,500
But the gods have spun no such happiness for me —

154
00:10:56,500 --> 00:11:01,280
not for my father, not for me.
Now, come what may, I must endure."

155
00:11:01,280 --> 00:11:03,920
Then Gerenian Nestor the horseman answered him:

156
00:11:03,920 --> 00:11:07,220
"Dear boy — since you say it
yourself and bring it to my mind —

157
00:11:07,220 --> 00:11:12,920
they tell me many suitors for your mother's sake
fill your halls against your will, devising evils.

158
00:11:12,920 --> 00:11:15,420
Tell me: do you bow to it willingly, or do the people

159
00:11:15,420 --> 00:11:18,680
through the land hate you, following the voice of a god?

160
00:11:18,680 --> 00:11:22,280
Who knows whether one day he will
come and repay their violence —

161
00:11:22,280 --> 00:11:25,220
alone, it may be, or all the Achaeans with him?

162
00:11:25,220 --> 00:11:30,100
If only gray-eyed Athena chose to love you the
way she cared in those days for glorious Odysseus

163
00:11:30,100 --> 00:11:33,560
in the Trojans' country,
where we Achaeans suffered our pains —

164
00:11:33,560 --> 00:11:39,040
never have I seen the gods love a man so openly
as Pallas Athena stood by him, in the open —

165
00:11:39,040 --> 00:11:42,400
if she chose to love you like that,
and care for you in her heart,

166
00:11:42,400 --> 00:11:45,940
then more than one of those men would
forget about marriage for good."

167
00:11:45,940 --> 00:11:48,720
Then clear-headed Telemachus answered him:

168
00:11:48,720 --> 00:11:52,780
"Old sir, I do not think that
word will ever be accomplished.

169
00:11:52,780 --> 00:11:56,360
What you say is too great. Awe holds me. I could not

170
00:11:56,360 --> 00:12:00,120
hope for it to happen —
not if the gods themselves willed it."

171
00:12:00,120 --> 00:12:03,860
Then the goddess spoke to him again, gray-eyed Athena:

172
00:12:03,860 --> 00:12:06,900
"Telemachus, what a word has
slipped the fence of your teeth!

173
00:12:06,900 --> 00:12:11,720
Easily, if he wills it, a god brings
a man safe home — even from far away.

174
00:12:11,720 --> 00:12:17,300
And I would rather suffer pains in plenty first and
then come home, and look on my day of homecoming,

175
00:12:17,300 --> 00:12:23,820
than come and die at my own hearth, as Agamemnon died,
by the treachery of Aegisthus and of his own wife.

176
00:12:23,820 --> 00:12:27,160
But death — the death common to all — not even the gods

177
00:12:27,160 --> 00:12:29,960
can beat away from a man they love, once the day comes

178
00:12:29,960 --> 00:12:33,440
when the ruinous doom of death
that lays men low takes hold."

179
00:12:33,440 --> 00:12:36,860
Then clear-headed Telemachus answered her:

180
00:12:36,860 --> 00:12:41,120
"Mentor, let us speak of it no
longer, grieved though we are.

181
00:12:41,120 --> 00:12:48,180
For him no homecoming is real any more: long since the
deathless ones have plotted his death and black doom.

182
00:12:48,180 --> 00:12:54,240
Now there is another word I want to ask, of Nestor —
since beyond other men he knows judgments and wisdom;

183
00:12:54,240 --> 00:12:57,040
three generations of men, they say, he has ruled as king,

184
00:12:57,040 --> 00:13:00,820
and to my eyes he seems like one of the deathless ones.

185
00:13:00,820 --> 00:13:07,780
Nestor, son of Neleus: tell me the truth of it —
how did Atreus' son die, wide-ruling Agamemnon?

186
00:13:07,780 --> 00:13:11,800
Where was Menelaus? What death
did the schemer plot for him,

187
00:13:11,800 --> 00:13:15,980
Aegisthus of the crooked counsels —
since the man he killed was far the better?

188
00:13:15,980 --> 00:13:19,020
Was Menelaus not in Achaean Argos — was he wandering

189
00:13:19,020 --> 00:13:22,080
somewhere among men, that the
other took heart and killed?"

190
00:13:22,080 --> 00:13:25,920
Then Gerenian Nestor the horseman answered him:

191
00:13:25,920 --> 00:13:29,320
"Then I will tell you, child, the whole truth of it.

192
00:13:29,320 --> 00:13:36,100
You have guessed yourself how it would have gone,
if Atreus' son, fair-haired Menelaus, coming from Troy,

193
00:13:36,100 --> 00:13:39,240
had found Aegisthus alive in the halls:

194
00:13:39,240 --> 00:13:44,580
no man would have heaped the mounded earth over
his dead body — dogs and birds would have torn him

195
00:13:44,580 --> 00:13:47,940
as he lay on the plain far from the city, and not one

196
00:13:47,940 --> 00:13:52,600
of the Achaean women would have wept him:
too monstrous the thing he schemed.

197
00:13:52,600 --> 00:13:59,500
For we were over there, winning our many contests —
and he, at his ease in a fold of horse-grazing Argos,

198
00:13:59,500 --> 00:14:04,020
worked all the while at Agamemnon's
wife with charming words.

199
00:14:04,020 --> 00:14:10,580
At first she pushed the shameful thing away —
shining Clytemnestra — for her mind was still sound;

200
00:14:10,580 --> 00:14:13,580
and beside her, too, there was a singer, whom Atreus' son

201
00:14:13,580 --> 00:14:16,060
strictly charged, when he left
for Troy, to guard his wife.

202
00:14:16,060 --> 00:14:23,060
But when the doom of the gods bound her to her undoing,
then Aegisthus led that singer to a desert island

203
00:14:23,060 --> 00:14:26,840
and left him there, spoil and carrion for the birds,

204
00:14:26,840 --> 00:14:31,120
and led her — he willing,
she willing — home to his own house.

205
00:14:31,120 --> 00:14:37,580
Many thigh-cuts he burned on the gods' holy altars,
many offerings he hung up, woven things and gold,

206
00:14:37,580 --> 00:14:40,680
having accomplished the enormity
his heart had never hoped for.

207
00:14:40,680 --> 00:14:46,900
Now we had sailed together on the way from Troy,
Atreus' son and I, in all friendship;

208
00:14:46,900 --> 00:14:52,900
but when we reached holy Sunium, the headland of Athens,
there Phoebus Apollo came with his gentle arrows

209
00:14:52,900 --> 00:14:58,880
and killed the helmsman of Menelaus,
the steering-oar of the running ship still in his hands:

210
00:14:58,880 --> 00:15:05,160
Phrontis, Onetor's son, who outdid all the tribes of
men at steering a ship when the storm-winds drive.

211
00:15:05,160 --> 00:15:08,840
So Menelaus was held there,
pressed though he was for the road,

212
00:15:08,840 --> 00:15:12,280
to bury his companion and pay the death-rites over him.

213
00:15:12,280 --> 00:15:17,260
But when he too, going on over the wine-dark sea in
the hollow ships, came at a run to the steep height

214
00:15:17,260 --> 00:15:23,860
of Malea, then wide-voiced Zeus devised for him a
hateful road: he poured on the breath of screaming winds

215
00:15:23,860 --> 00:15:27,740
and monstrous waves, swollen huge as mountains.

216
00:15:27,740 --> 00:15:31,120
There he split the fleet, and part he drove on Crete,

217
00:15:31,120 --> 00:15:34,300
where the Cydonians lived around the streams of Iardanus.

218
00:15:34,300 --> 00:15:39,900
There is a bare cliff, sheer to the salt water,
at the edge of Gortyn on the misted sea,

219
00:15:39,900 --> 00:15:43,360
where the South Wind shoves the
great swell on the left headland,

220
00:15:43,360 --> 00:15:47,840
toward Phaestus, and a little
stone holds back the great sea.

221
00:15:47,840 --> 00:15:50,880
There part of them came in,
and the men escaped destruction

222
00:15:50,880 --> 00:15:57,180
barely, but the waves smashed the ships on the reefs;
and the other five ships with their dark-blue prows

223
00:15:57,180 --> 00:16:00,440
the wind and the water carried and beached on Egypt.

224
00:16:00,440 --> 00:16:05,940
So Menelaus, gathering much substance and gold,
roved with his ships among men of alien speech —

225
00:16:05,940 --> 00:16:10,020
and in that very time Aegisthus
schemed those horrors at home.

226
00:16:10,020 --> 00:16:12,880
Seven years he reigned over Mycenae rich in gold,

227
00:16:12,880 --> 00:16:16,600
having killed Atreus' son,
and the people were broken under him;

228
00:16:16,600 --> 00:16:22,500
but in the eighth his evil came on him: noble Orestes,
back from Athens, cut down his father's killer —

229
00:16:22,500 --> 00:16:26,420
Aegisthus of the crooked counsels,
who cut down his famous father.

230
00:16:26,420 --> 00:16:32,260
The killing done, he gave the Argives a funeral feast
for his hateful mother and for spineless Aegisthus;

231
00:16:32,260 --> 00:16:35,880
and on that same day Menelaus of
the great war-cry reached him,

232
00:16:35,880 --> 00:16:39,860
bringing home treasure,
all the freight his ships could bear.

233
00:16:39,860 --> 00:16:46,060
You too, my friend: do not wander long and far from home,
leaving your goods behind and men in your house

234
00:16:46,060 --> 00:16:50,180
of such overbearing insolence —
or they will devour it all,

235
00:16:50,180 --> 00:16:54,180
dividing your goods among them,
and your journey come to nothing.

236
00:16:54,180 --> 00:16:57,340
But to Menelaus I bid you go — I urge it:

237
00:16:57,340 --> 00:17:01,800
he is newly home from foreign parts,
from among peoples no man would hope in his heart

238
00:17:01,800 --> 00:17:07,780
to return from, once the storm-winds had driven him
astray into a sea so vast that not even the birds

239
00:17:07,780 --> 00:17:16,380
can cross it in a year — so vast it is, and terrible.

240
00:17:16,380 --> 00:17:20,040
Go now, with your own ship and your companions;

241
00:17:20,040 --> 00:17:24,400
or if you would rather go by land,
chariot and horses are here,

242
00:17:24,400 --> 00:17:31,760
and here are my sons, who will be your escorts to
shining Lacedaemon, where fair-haired Menelaus is.

243
00:17:31,760 --> 00:17:35,460
Beg him yourself, so that he speaks unerring truth.

244
00:17:35,460 --> 00:17:40,180
He will tell you no lie:
he is clear-headed beyond most men."

245
00:17:40,180 --> 00:17:43,740
So he spoke; and the sun went down, and the dusk came on.

246
00:17:43,740 --> 00:17:47,120
And among them spoke the goddess, gray-eyed Athena:

247
00:17:47,120 --> 00:17:51,460
"Old sir, all this you have told rightly, in due measure.

248
00:17:51,460 --> 00:17:57,700
But come — cut up the tongues now, and mix the wine,
so that we may pour to Poseidon and the other immortals

249
00:17:57,700 --> 00:18:00,680
and then think of sleep. It is the hour for it:

250
00:18:00,680 --> 00:18:04,600
the light is gone already under the
western gloom, and it is not fitting

251
00:18:04,600 --> 00:18:08,680
to sit long at the feast of the gods, but to go home."

252
00:18:08,680 --> 00:18:12,420
So spoke the daughter of Zeus, and they heard her voice.

253
00:18:12,420 --> 00:18:16,480
Heralds poured water over their hands,
and young men crowned the mixing-bowls with drink

254
00:18:16,480 --> 00:18:20,420
and served all, pouring first
drops in each cup for the gods;

255
00:18:20,420 --> 00:18:24,840
and they threw the tongues in the fire,
and stood, and poured above them.

256
00:18:24,840 --> 00:18:30,140
But when they had poured, and drunk what their
hearts would have, then Athena and godlike Telemachus

257
00:18:30,140 --> 00:18:32,700
both made to go back to the hollow ship.

258
00:18:32,700 --> 00:18:36,080
But Nestor held them back,
catching at them with his words:

259
00:18:36,080 --> 00:18:40,800
"Zeus forbid it — and the other deathless gods —
that you should go from my house to your fast ship

260
00:18:40,800 --> 00:18:47,220
as if from some man utterly cloakless and poor,
who has no cloaks, no plenty of blankets in his house,

261
00:18:47,220 --> 00:18:50,520
no soft sleeping for himself or for his guests!

262
00:18:50,520 --> 00:18:53,480
I have cloaks and beautiful blankets in plenty.

263
00:18:53,480 --> 00:18:59,260
Never, while I live, shall the dear son of this man
Odysseus lie down on a ship's deck-planks — nor after,

264
00:18:59,260 --> 00:19:04,100
while sons of mine are left in the halls to
host whatever guest arrives at my doors."

265
00:19:04,100 --> 00:19:07,660
Then the goddess spoke to him again, gray-eyed Athena:

266
00:19:07,660 --> 00:19:12,780
"Well said, dear old friend — and it is fitting
that Telemachus obey you: far better so.

267
00:19:12,780 --> 00:19:17,360
Let him go with you now, then, and sleep in
your halls. But I will go to the black ship

268
00:19:17,360 --> 00:19:20,380
to hearten the companions and give them all their orders.

269
00:19:20,380 --> 00:19:22,900
For I may claim to be the only elder among them:

270
00:19:22,900 --> 00:19:28,240
the rest are younger men who follow out of friendship,
all of an age with great-hearted Telemachus.

271
00:19:28,240 --> 00:19:31,300
There let me lie down tonight, by the black hollow ship;

272
00:19:31,300 --> 00:19:36,820
and in the morning I go on to the great-hearted
Cauconians, where a debt is owed me — no new thing

273
00:19:36,820 --> 00:19:40,940
and no small one. But this man,
since he has come to your house,

274
00:19:40,940 --> 00:19:47,220
send on with a chariot and a son of yours; and give
him horses, your lightest runners, your strongest."

275
00:19:47,220 --> 00:19:53,460
So speaking, gray-eyed Athena went away in the likeness
of a sea-eagle; and amazement took all who saw,

276
00:19:53,460 --> 00:19:56,480
and the old man marveled, when his eyes had seen it.

277
00:19:56,480 --> 00:20:00,320
He took Telemachus' hand, and spoke a word, and named him:

278
00:20:00,320 --> 00:20:04,320
"Dear friend, I have no fear you
will prove base or spineless,

279
00:20:04,320 --> 00:20:08,880
if the gods walk with you so, escorts, and you so young!

280
00:20:08,880 --> 00:20:15,020
For this was none other of those who hold Olympus
than the daughter of Zeus, most glorious Tritogeneia,

281
00:20:15,020 --> 00:20:18,040
she who honored your noble father too among the Argives.

282
00:20:18,040 --> 00:20:23,660
Queen, be gracious! Grant me good fame —
to me, to my children, to my honored wife —

283
00:20:23,660 --> 00:20:27,460
and I in turn will offer you a
yearling heifer, broad of brow,

284
00:20:27,460 --> 00:20:30,520
unbroken, that no man has ever led under the yoke:

285
00:20:30,520 --> 00:20:34,360
her I will offer to you,
with gold poured round her horns."

286
00:20:34,360 --> 00:20:37,600
So he spoke in prayer, and Pallas Athena heard him.

287
00:20:37,600 --> 00:20:45,060
Then Gerenian Nestor the horseman led them — his sons
and his daughters' husbands — to his beautiful house.

288
00:20:45,060 --> 00:20:51,000
And when they reached the lord's famous house,
they sat in order on the benches and the high-backed chairs,

289
00:20:51,000 --> 00:20:56,480
and for their coming the old man mixed a bowl
of sweet-drinking wine, eleven years old,

290
00:20:56,480 --> 00:21:00,540
which the woman-steward opened, loosening the sealing cap.

291
00:21:00,540 --> 00:21:03,420
Of this the old man mixed a bowl, and to Athena

292
00:21:03,420 --> 00:21:08,180
he prayed long, pouring,
to the daughter of aegis-bearing Zeus.

293
00:21:08,180 --> 00:21:11,300
But when they had poured,
and drunk what their hearts would have,

294
00:21:11,300 --> 00:21:14,900
the others went home to sleep, each man to his own house,

295
00:21:14,900 --> 00:21:21,400
but Gerenian Nestor the horseman laid him down there —
Telemachus, dear son of godlike Odysseus —

296
00:21:21,400 --> 00:21:24,420
on a corded bedstead under the echoing porch,

297
00:21:24,420 --> 00:21:31,520
and beside him Peisistratus of the good ash spear,
leader of men, the one son in his halls still unwed;

298
00:21:31,520 --> 00:21:34,620
and he himself slept in the inner room of the high house,

299
00:21:34,620 --> 00:21:38,420
where the lady his wife made ready his bed and his rest.

300
00:21:38,420 --> 00:21:45,200
When Dawn appeared, the early-born, with fingers of rose,
Gerenian Nestor the horseman rose up from his bed,

301
00:21:45,200 --> 00:21:50,620
and came out, and took his seat on the polished
stones that stood before his high doors,

302
00:21:50,620 --> 00:21:54,500
white, gleaming as if with oil. On these, in the old days,

303
00:21:54,500 --> 00:21:57,580
Neleus used to sit, a counselor the equal of the gods;

304
00:21:57,580 --> 00:22:01,500
but he long since had gone down to Hades, broken by fate,

305
00:22:01,500 --> 00:22:05,880
and now Gerenian Nestor sat there
in his turn, warder of the Achaeans,

306
00:22:05,880 --> 00:22:09,620
holding the scepter. Around him
his sons gathered in a body,

307
00:22:09,620 --> 00:22:15,920
coming from their rooms: Echephron and Stratius,
Perseus and Aretus and godlike Thrasymedes;

308
00:22:15,920 --> 00:22:18,920
and sixth came the hero Peisistratus to join them,

309
00:22:18,920 --> 00:22:22,460
and they led godlike Telemachus
and seated him beside them.

310
00:22:22,460 --> 00:22:25,640
And Gerenian Nestor the horseman
began to speak among them:

311
00:22:25,640 --> 00:22:31,660
"Quickly, dear children, work my wish for me,
so that first of all the gods I may propitiate Athena,

312
00:22:31,660 --> 00:22:34,840
who came to me in visible
presence at the god's rich feast.

313
00:22:34,840 --> 00:22:40,800
Come then: one of you to the plain for a heifer,
fast as may be, and let the cowherd drive her in;

314
00:22:40,800 --> 00:22:46,780
one go down to great-hearted Telemachus' black ship
and bring up all his companions — leave two men only;

315
00:22:46,780 --> 00:22:51,680
one bid the goldsmith Laerces come here
to pour gold round the heifer's horns.

316
00:22:51,680 --> 00:22:54,800
The rest of you stay here together;
and tell the slave-women

317
00:22:54,800 --> 00:23:01,760
inside to make ready a feast through the famous house —
seats, and firewood round about, and shining water."

318
00:23:01,760 --> 00:23:08,960
So he spoke, and they all fell to bustling. The heifer
came up from the plain; from the fast trim ship came

319
00:23:08,960 --> 00:23:12,560
great-hearted Telemachus' companions; the smith came

320
00:23:12,560 --> 00:23:16,640
with his bronze tools in his hands,
the instruments of his art,

321
00:23:16,640 --> 00:23:21,800
anvil and hammer and the well-made fire-tongs
he worked the gold with; and Athena came

322
00:23:21,800 --> 00:23:28,040
to receive her offering. And the old horseman Nestor
gave gold, and the smith worked it and poured it round

323
00:23:28,040 --> 00:23:33,200
the heifer's horns, so the goddess
might see the glory and be glad.

324
00:23:33,200 --> 00:23:39,100
Stratius and noble Echephron led her by the horns;
Aretus came from the storeroom carrying lustral water

325
00:23:39,100 --> 00:23:42,420
in a flowered basin, and in his
other hand the barley-grains

326
00:23:42,420 --> 00:23:46,560
in a basket; and Thrasymedes,
steadfast in battle, stood by

327
00:23:46,560 --> 00:23:49,620
with a sharp axe in his hand, to strike the heifer down;

328
00:23:49,620 --> 00:23:53,460
Perseus held the blood-bowl. And the old horseman Nestor

329
00:23:53,460 --> 00:23:57,140
began with the hand-water and the
barley-grains, and long to Athena

330
00:23:57,140 --> 00:24:02,520
he prayed, making first offering,
casting hairs from the head into the fire.

331
00:24:02,520 --> 00:24:05,520
And when they had prayed and thrown the scattering barley,

332
00:24:05,520 --> 00:24:11,360
straightway Nestor's son, high-spirited Thrasymedes,
stood close and struck: the axe cut through the tendons

333
00:24:11,360 --> 00:24:16,100
of the neck, and undid the heifer's
strength. And the women cried aloud —

334
00:24:16,100 --> 00:24:18,520
the daughters and the sons' wives and the honored wife

335
00:24:18,520 --> 00:24:23,080
of Nestor, Eurydice, eldest of the daughters of Clymenus.

336
00:24:23,080 --> 00:24:25,340
Then the men lifted her up from the wide-wayed earth

337
00:24:25,340 --> 00:24:29,640
and held her, and Peisistratus,
leader of men, cut the throat.

338
00:24:29,640 --> 00:24:33,460
And when the black blood had run out,
and the life had left the bones,

339
00:24:33,460 --> 00:24:36,760
quickly they broke her apart,
and at once cut out the thigh-pieces

340
00:24:36,760 --> 00:24:42,800
all in due order, and wrapped them deep in fat,
folding it double, and laid raw flesh upon them.

341
00:24:42,800 --> 00:24:47,000
The old man burned them on the split wood,
and poured the fire-bright wine

342
00:24:47,000 --> 00:24:50,720
over; and the young men beside
him held the five-tined forks.

343
00:24:50,720 --> 00:24:54,140
And when the thighs were burned through,
and they had tasted the innards,

344
00:24:54,140 --> 00:24:59,800
they carved up the rest and threaded it on spits and
roasted it, holding the sharp spits in their hands.

345
00:24:59,800 --> 00:25:05,320
Meanwhile beautiful Polycaste had bathed Telemachus —
the youngest daughter of Nestor, Neleus' son.

346
00:25:05,320 --> 00:25:08,060
And when she had bathed him
and rubbed him richly with oil,

347
00:25:08,060 --> 00:25:11,280
she threw a beautiful mantle around him, and a tunic,

348
00:25:11,280 --> 00:25:14,020
and he stepped from the bath with the body of an immortal,

349
00:25:14,020 --> 00:25:17,320
and went and sat down beside Nestor,
shepherd of the people.

350
00:25:17,320 --> 00:25:20,100
And when they had roasted the
outer meats and drawn them off,

351
00:25:20,100 --> 00:25:25,500
they sat and feasted; and good men waited
on them, pouring wine into cups of gold.

352
00:25:25,500 --> 00:25:28,720
But when they had put away desire for food and drink,

353
00:25:28,720 --> 00:25:31,500
Gerenian Nestor the horseman began to speak among them:

354
00:25:31,500 --> 00:25:35,340
"My sons, come — yoke the
fine-maned horses for Telemachus,

355
00:25:35,340 --> 00:25:38,180
lead them under the chariot, so he may be making his way."

356
00:25:38,180 --> 00:25:41,560
So he spoke, and they listened well and obeyed:

357
00:25:41,560 --> 00:25:46,600
quickly they yoked the swift horses under the chariot,
and the woman-steward put in bread and wine

358
00:25:46,600 --> 00:25:50,840
and cooked meats, such things as Zeus-nurtured kings eat.

359
00:25:50,840 --> 00:25:57,580
Then Telemachus mounted the beautiful chariot,
and beside him Peisistratus, Nestor's son, leader of men,

360
00:25:57,580 --> 00:25:59,900
mounted the chariot and took the reins in his hands,

361
00:25:59,900 --> 00:26:03,720
and whipped them to a run;
and the pair flew on, nothing loath,

362
00:26:03,720 --> 00:26:07,640
into the plain, and left the steep city of Pylos behind.

363
00:26:07,640 --> 00:26:10,840
All day long they shook the yoke they bore between them.

364
00:26:10,840 --> 00:26:16,840
The sun went down, and all the ways grew dark,
and they came to Pherae, to the house of Diocles,

365
00:26:16,840 --> 00:26:20,140
son of Ortilochus, whom Alpheus fathered.

366
00:26:20,140 --> 00:26:23,520
There they slept the night,
and he set guest-gifts before them.

367
00:26:23,520 --> 00:26:27,280
When Dawn appeared, the early-born, with fingers of rose,

368
00:26:27,280 --> 00:26:33,560
they yoked the horses and mounted the inlaid chariot
and drove out of the gateway and the echoing porch;

369
00:26:33,560 --> 00:26:37,840
and he whipped them to a run,
and the pair flew on, nothing loath.

370
00:26:37,840 --> 00:26:40,800
They came to the wheat-bearing
plain, and there they pressed

371
00:26:40,800 --> 00:26:45,060
to the journey's end: so well
the swift horses carried them.

372
00:26:45,060 --> 00:26:47,856
The sun went down, and all the ways grew dark.
